{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "In `for i in names:`, what is i on each pass?",
      "options": [
        "One element of names, the next one each time",
        "The position of an element, 0, 1, 2 and so on",
        "The whole list names",
        "How many elements names has"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "What is i on the first pass?",
      "code": "names = [\"Tommy\", \"Lance\", \"Cortez\"]\nfor i in names:\n    print(f\"Hello, {i}!\")",
      "options": ["0", "\"Lance\"", "\"Tommy\"", "[\"Tommy\", \"Lance\", \"Cortez\"]"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "A list has 4 elements. How many times do the lines under `for x in the_list:` run?",
      "options": ["3", "5", "Once", "4"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "names has 3 elements. Which numbers does `range(0, len(names))` give?",
      "options": ["1, 2, 3", "3", "0, 1, 2, 3", "0, 1, 2"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "In `for i in range(len(names)):`, what is i?",
      "options": [
        "An index, a position in the list",
        "A name from the list",
        "The length of the list",
        "The list itself"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "Why does the lesson print `i + 1` next to each hero?",
      "options": [
        "Because range() starts from 1",
        "So the loop skips the first hero",
        "We count from 0 in code, but people count from 1",
        "Because i + 1 is the next hero's name"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "When do you need `for i in range(len(names)):` and not `for n in names:`?",
      "options": [
        "When you need the position of each element too",
        "Always, the second one doesn't work on lists",
        "When the list holds numbers",
        "When the list is empty"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "names = [\"Tommy\", \"Lance\"]\nfor n in names:\n    print(n[0])",
